    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3013.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 723,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water is a chemical compound made of hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the very foundation of life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique. In fact, it is one of the strangest and most miraculous substances in the universe. \n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (water), **solid** (ice), and **gas** (steam or water vapor).\n* **Universal Solvent:** Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it dissolves more substances than any other liquid. This ability is what allows water to carry vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through living systems and the environment.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* when it turns to ice. This means ice is less dense than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nEarth is often called the **\"Blue Planet\"** because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **Where it is:** The vast majority of Earth's water (about 96.5%) is saline water found in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is freshwater. \n* **Locked away:** Most of that freshwater is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a closed loop called the hydrological cycle. It evaporates from the oceans and land, rises into the atmosphere to form clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows into rivers and oceans to start the process over again. The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago.\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nBiology cannot exist without water. \n* **Inside Us:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. The brain is about 73% water, the lungs are about 83%, and even bones are about 31% water. \n* **Cellular Fuel:** Inside living organisms, water acts as the medium for almost all metabolic and cellular processes. It flushes out toxins, regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, and transports oxygen and nutrients through the bloodstream.\n* **Origin of Life:** Scientists believe that life itself originated in the watery depths of Earth's primordial oceans around 4 billion years ago.\n\n### Summary\nWater is a simple molecule with extraordinary properties. It sculpts the landscapes of our planet, drives our weather and climate, and flows through the veins of every living thing. Without it, life as we know it would simply cease to exist."
